**Assessment**
- Exposure profile: `material_exposure`.
- Modi was the Prime Minister in 2022, the PM chaired the Nuclear Command Authority political council, and the PMO/DAE structure gives the office direct control over atomic-energy policy. The public 2022 record shows restraint and continuity, not a doctrine rewrite.
- Inherited posture: India already had `credible minimum deterrence`, `no-first-use`, civilian control, and a survivable triad before 2022.
- End-2022 posture: still largely the same doctrine, with continued modernization and survivable retaliatory capability, plus repeated diplomatic restraint on Ukraine/Russia.

**Reused Evidence**
- None from prior web research. I used the local structured prior as the starting baseline, but there are no accepted web evidence IDs to reuse yet.

**New Evidence**
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Know the PM","publisher":"Prime Minister of India","publication_date":"2026-07-22","url":"https://www.pmindia.gov.in/en/pms-profile/","claim":"The official PMO biography states that Narendra Modi served as Prime Minister from 2019 to 2024, so he was in office throughout 2022.","locator":"biography paragraph with the service dates","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official government biography; direct office-tenure claim.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Confirms officeholding during the target year.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributes executive office to Modi, but not any specific nuclear decision.","contrary_evidence":["Promotional tone and no detail on nuclear authority or 2022 conduct."],"lenses":["1B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"History","publisher":"Department Of Atomic Energy, India","publication_date":"2026-07-22","url":"https://dae.gov.in/history/","claim":"The Department of Atomic Energy says it was set up under the direct charge of the Prime Minister, which makes Modi's office directly relevant to atomic-energy governance in 2022.","locator":"'About Department' paragraph","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official government department history page; direct institutional authority claim.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Long-running institutional structure that still applies in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Institutional authority attaches to the PM office, not to a specific personal act by Modi.","contrary_evidence":["This is structural authority, not evidence of a 2022 risk-bearing choice."],"lenses":["1B.3","1B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"NCA reviews nuclear capability","publisher":"Press Information Bureau / Prime Minister's Office","publication_date":"2004-10-14","url":"https://www.pib.gov.in/newsite/erelcontent.aspx?lang=2&reg=48&relid=4371","claim":"The Nuclear Command Authority under the Prime Minister's chairmanship reviewed India's nuclear capability and said it had command and control structures in place.","locator":"opening paragraph of the press release","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official government press release naming the PM as NCA chair.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Inherited command structure that remained relevant in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Shows the PM's formal nuclear authority; the 2004 text predates Modi but defines the office he later occupied.","contrary_evidence":["Old source, so it establishes inherited structure rather than 2022 conduct."],"lenses":["1B.3","1B.7","1B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Prime Minister felicitates crew of INS Arihant on completion of Nuclear Triad","publisher":"Press Information Bureau / Prime Minister's Office","publication_date":"2018","url":"https://www.pib.gov.in/Pressreleaseshare.aspx?PRID=1551894","claim":"The PMO said India had put in place a robust nuclear command and control structure, effective safety assurance architecture, and strict political control under the Nuclear Command Authority, while remaining committed to credible minimum deterrence and no-first-use.","locator":"main body of the press release","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official PMO text on command-and-control and doctrine.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Inherited baseline for 2022.","ruler_attribution":"This is inherited doctrine and structure, not a 2022 Modi-specific action.","contrary_evidence":["It is a favorable self-description and does not prove operational perfection."],"lenses":["1B.3","1B.4","1B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"INS Arihant carries out successful launch of Submarine Launched Ballistic Missile","publisher":"Press Information Bureau / Ministry of Defence","publication_date":"2022-10-14","url":"https://www.pib.gov.in/Pressreleaseshare.aspx?PRID=1867778","claim":"India said the Arihant SLBM launch validated the SSBN programme and a robust survivable assured retaliatory capability consistent with its no-first-use posture.","locator":"second and third paragraphs of the release","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official 2022 government release on a nuclear-capability event.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within the target year.","ruler_attribution":"Government-level capability maintenance under Modi; it does not show a crisis decision or doctrinal change by him personally.","contrary_evidence":["Strengthening retaliatory capability can stabilize deterrence, but it also continues modernization rather than disarmament."],"lenses":["1B.3","1B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Nuclear weapons at a glance: India and Pakistan","publisher":"House of Commons Library","publication_date":"2022-07-29","url":"https://commonslibrary.parliament.uk/research-briefings/cbp-9070/","claim":"The 2022 parliamentary briefing says India is outside the NPT and CTBT, maintains a no-first-use policy, continues to produce weapons-grade fissile material, is estimated at about 160 warheads, and is actively expanding delivery capabilities.","locator":"India section bullet list","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Parliamentary research briefing synthesizing multiple public sources.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly relevant to the 2022 posture and inherited baseline.","ruler_attribution":"Country-level nuclear posture under Modi's tenure, but not proof of any single personal decision.","contrary_evidence":["It is a synthesis rather than a primary Indian source.","It describes capability trends, not operational misuse."],"lenses":["1B.4","1B.5","1B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Nuclear Notebook: How many nuclear weapons does India have in 2022?","publisher":"Bulletin of the Atomic Scientists","publication_date":"2022-07-11","url":"https://thebulletin.org/premium/2022-07/nuclear-notebook-how-many-nuclear-weapons-does-india-have-in-2022/","claim":"The 2022 Nuclear Notebook estimates India at about 160 warheads, says at least four new systems are under development, and notes additional plutonium capacity plus a growing emphasis on China.","locator":"opening summary and Table 1","source_type":"scholarship","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Authoritative technical nuclear-arsenal assessment by FAS experts published in the Bulletin.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Direct 2022 arsenal and modernization context.","ruler_attribution":"Shows the inherited and continuing posture Modi governed in 2022, but not a personal crisis choice.","contrary_evidence":["Estimate-based, not an official inventory.","Modernization is not itself evidence of reckless use."],"lenses":["1B.3","1B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"प्रधानमंत्री रूस के राष्ट्रपति के साथ द्विपक्षीय बैठक में प्रधानमंत्री श्री नरेन्द्र मोदी का उद्घाटन भाषण","publisher":"Press Information Bureau / Prime Minister's Office","publication_date":"2022-09-16","url":"https://www.pib.gov.in/PressReleasePage.aspx?PRID=1859971","claim":"In the original Hindi release, Modi told Putin that this is not an era of war and that democracy, diplomacy, and dialogue are the path forward.","locator":"opening remarks in Hindi","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary local-language government text from the target year.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within the target year and directly about Modi.","ruler_attribution":"Direct ruler speech at a major geopolitical crisis moment.","contrary_evidence":["This is a diplomatic statement, not a nuclear doctrine change or command-control reform."],"lenses":["1B.2","1B.8"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"No Modi-Putin summit this year after they met in September- Indian govt source","publisher":"Reuters via Euronews","publication_date":"2022-12-10","url":"https://www.euronews.com/2022/12/10/india-russia-modi","claim":"Reuters reported that India did not explicitly condemn Russia's invasion, remained a major buyer of Russian oil, and that the decision not to hold the annual Modi-Putin summit was not due to the nuclear angle.","locator":"Reuters report text near the lead and second paragraphs","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters reporting carried by Euronews, with an explicit government-source attribution inside the report.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Covers late-2022 statecraft around the Ukraine war.","ruler_attribution":"Reports on Modi's broader diplomatic balancing, with one factual sentence directly attributed to an Indian government source.","contrary_evidence":["India stayed strategically close to Russia on trade and energy even while urging restraint."],"lenses":["1B.2","1B.8"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Visit of the Prime Minister of the United Kingdom to India (April 21-22, 2022)","publisher":"Prime Minister of India","publication_date":"2022-04-22","url":"https://www.pmindia.gov.in/en/news_updates/visit-of-the-prime-minister-of-the-united-kingdom-to-india-april-21-22-2022/","claim":"Modi said the Ukraine-Russia conflict required an immediate cessation of violence and a return to direct dialogue and diplomacy as the only way forward.","locator":"paragraph 7 of the joint statement","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official bilateral statement on the PMO site, dated in the target year.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 statement during the target year.","ruler_attribution":"Direct ruler-level diplomatic position.","contrary_evidence":["Again, this is rhetorical restraint rather than a demonstrable nuclear-risk reduction measure."],"lenses":["1B.2","1B.8"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Prime Minister Shri Narendra Modi speaks on telephone with His Excellency Mr Vladimir Putin, President of the Russian Federation","publisher":"Press Information Bureau / Prime Minister's Office","publication_date":"2022-12-16","url":"https://www.pib.gov.in/Pressreleaseshare.aspx?PRID=1884133&lang=2&reg=48","claim":"The PMO said Modi reiterated that dialogue and diplomacy were the only way forward in the Ukraine conflict.","locator":"third paragraph of the release","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official PMO readout of the call, dated in the target year.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within the target year.","ruler_attribution":"Direct ruler-level crisis messaging to a nuclear-armed counterpart.","contrary_evidence":["It is a peace-oriented call, not proof of changes to command control or treaty posture."],"lenses":["1B.2","1B.8"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"First Committee Sends 20 Nuclear Weapons Drafts to General Assembly Requiring 33 Separate Votes for Adoption","publisher":"United Nations Press","publication_date":"2022-10-28","url":"https://press.un.org/en/2022/gadis3701.doc.htm","claim":"The UN First Committee record shows India abstained on the 2022 CTBT-related nuclear-disarmament text and on the broader nuclear-disarmament draft that called for a legally binding no-first-use instrument.","locator":"paragraphs describing the CTBT vote and the overall nuclear-disarmament draft","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous UN voting record with explicit country vote details.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within the target year.","ruler_attribution":"This is the government's multilateral position under Modi, but not a personal statement by him.","contrary_evidence":["India continued to advocate disarmament language even while withholding support from specific texts."],"lenses":["1B.4","1B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Statement by Ambassador DB Venkatesh Varma Permanent Representative of India to the CD during the Thematic Debate on Nuclear Weapons at First Committee","publisher":"Permanent Mission of India to the Conference on Disarmament in Geneva","publication_date":"2022-10-17","url":"https://pmindiaun.gov.in/Cdgeneva/statement_content/MjEw","claim":"India said it remained unwaveringly committed to universal, non-discriminatory, verifiable nuclear disarmament, supported a step-by-step multilateral framework, and backed measures to reduce nuclear danger, including de-alerting and de-targeting.","locator":"opening and middle paragraphs of the statement","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official Indian UN statement reproduced on the mission site; directly dated to the target year.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 multilateral position.","ruler_attribution":"Government position under Modi; the statement is not personally by him but is attributable to his administration.","contrary_evidence":["It is longstanding doctrine language and does not by itself prove new 2022 risk reduction on the ground."],"lenses":["1B.1","1B.4","1B.6","1B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Statement by Mr. Maitrey Kulkarni, Deputy Secretary (D&ISA), Ministry of External Affairs, New Delhi at the CD Plenary on Aug 11, 2022 on 'The Centrality of International Cooperation and Capacity Building in Building Safe and Secure Cyberspace'","publisher":"Permanent Mission of India to the Conference on Disarmament in Geneva","publication_date":"2022-08-11","url":"https://pmindiaun.gov.in/Cdgeneva/statement_content/MTIz","claim":"India said international transfers of dual-use goods and high-technology with military applications should be effectively regulated, and that it remained committed to an open, secure, stable, accessible, and peaceful cyberspace.","locator":"paragraphs 2-3 of the statement text","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official 2022 Indian statement on an emerging-catastrophic-risk domain, reproduced by the mission site.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 evidence for dual-use and cyber governance.","ruler_attribution":"Government position under Modi; the speaker is an MEA official rather than Modi personally.","contrary_evidence":["This is policy language, not proof of effective implementation or transparency in practice."],"lenses":["1B.9","1B.7"]}

**Search And Rejection Summary**
- I reviewed roughly 30 documents across PMO/PIB, MEA, UN records, parliament briefings, technical nuclear assessments, and Reuters-carrying media.
- Retained 14 URLs, all of which added a distinct claim rather than a repeated paraphrase.
- Rejected as duplicates or repackaging: Reuters/Yahoo and other wire versions of the same Samarkand quote when the primary Hindi PMO release was already available; other copies of the same PM statement in different host sites.
- Rejected as out-of-period or retrospective for core evidence: 2019 Nuclear No First Use explainers, 2023-2026 retrospective doctrine pages, and later commentary that did not add target-year facts.
- Rejected as weak or too generic: broad India foreign-policy pages, unrelated economic pages, and general explainer/opinion pieces that repeated doctrine history without new 2022 evidence.
- Rejected because better primary evidence existed: commentary on nuclear doctrine, secondary summaries of the PM's Ukraine posture, and non-specific articles about India's foreign policy or parliamentary activity that did not materially change the 1B picture.

**Remaining Gaps**
- There is still no public 2022 evidence of any Modi-directed change to nuclear command procedures, custody audits, launch readiness, or internal NCA practices.
- There is no public, ruler-specific evidence of a 2022 decision about proliferation, command appointments, or expert staffing that would materially improve 1B.7.
- The record is strong on doctrine and diplomacy, but weak on operational safety and internal governance. That means the dossier supports restraint signals, but not a claim of exceptional risk reduction.
- If you want, the next useful pass would be a narrower search on 1B.3 and 1B.7: Cabinet/PMO appointment records, parliamentary written replies, and any declassified NCA/DAE material from 2022.
